      <div class="chart-src">Sources: the vendor study, the wire report and the downstream articles are all linked in the sources list below.</div>
    </div>

    <p>What survives that trace is worth having. A video tooling company published its own hand-categorised sample of the YouTube feed in November 2025, finding that a large share of a new account's first videos were AI-generated or low-effort, and it named specific channels. In late January 2026 a news wire reported that at least <strong>sixteen</strong> of those channels had been removed or emptied, including three with between 2.4 and 5.9 million subscribers. That report is an observation, not a confirmation: YouTube did not say it had removed them.</p>

    <p>Everything beyond that is unsafe. The revenue estimate traces to a content farm, the subscriber total travels only through outlets citing that farm, and the Verge investigation could not be located at all. It appears to be an attribution error introduced in one article and repeated ever since. The honest summary is that <strong>enforcement against low-effort AI channels is real and visible, and no official numbers for it exist.</strong></p>

    <div class="cav">The detection question</div>
    <h2 id="detect">How does YouTube detect AI content?</h2>
    <p>It largely does not need to, because it is not looking for AI. YouTube's stated approach is to build on <em>"established systems that have been very successful in combatting spam and clickbait"</em>, which are systems that assess behaviour and quality signals rather than provenance. There is no published detail of an AI detector, and no first-party enforcement statistics of any kind.</p>

    <p>That is reassuring in one direction and not in the other. A carefully made video with synthetic narration is not being hunted. A channel uploading interchangeable templated content is caught by rules that predate generative AI entirely, and would have been caught in 2019.</p>

    <div class="cav">Beyond YouTube</div>
    <h2 id="platforms">What about TikTok, Meta and the EU?</h2>
    <p>The rules differ more than people expect, and one video can be exempt on one platform and disclosable on another.</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Disclosure trigger</th><th>Position on another person's voice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="band">YouTube</td><td>Photorealistic depiction of real people, places or events</td><td>Own-voice cloning exempt from disclosure</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="band">TikTok</td><td>Realistic synthetic scenes, labelled by sticker or caption</td><td>Bans synthetic media carrying the likeness, visual or audio, of a real person</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="band">Meta</td><td>Originality rules are medium-agnostic; unoriginal content is deprioritized</td><td>Rules published without reference to AI in the 2026 originality update</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="band">EU AI Act</td><td>Deep fake audio or video, in force since 2 August 2026</td><td>Applies to deployers, with a reduced duty for evidently creative work</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The EU rule deserves care because it is new and widely misquoted. Article 50 requires that deployers of a system generating audio or video <em>"constituting a deep fake, shall disclose that the content has been artificially generated or manipulated."</em> A synthetic narrator over original animation is not obviously a deep fake; a cloned voice of a real person is. There is also a text provision that exempts content which <em>"has undergone a process of human review or editorial control"</em> where someone holds editorial responsibility, which describes any creator who edits their own script.</p>

    <div class="cav">When it goes wrong</div>
    <h2 id="appeal">What happens if your channel is rejected or demonetized?</h2>
    <p>There is a defined process with published timelines, and no published success rates. After a first rejection you can reapply <strong>30 days</strong> later; after any subsequent one, <strong>90 days</strong>. If a suspension is scheduled you can appeal within <strong>7 days</strong> before the date, or within <strong>21 days</strong> if it has already happened, and YouTube states its teams respond within <strong>14 days</strong>.</p>

    <p>What nobody can tell you honestly is how often appeals succeed, because neither YouTube nor any credible third party publishes that. Any article giving you a percentage invented it.</p>

    <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">The practical checklist</div><p>Write the script yourself or research it properly rather than reading someone else's page aloud. Vary the structure between videos rather than pouring every topic into one template. Do not build a persona presenting as a doctor, lawyer or financial adviser. Disclose when the content depicts something real that did not happen. Nothing on that list is about the voice.</p></div>

