      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Can you use an AI voice on YouTube Shorts?</div><div class="a">Yes, under the same rules as long-form video. There is no separate policy for synthetic voice in Shorts. The risks are the same and arguably sharper, because short repetitive formats are easier to mass-produce, which is precisely the pattern the generic and repetitive content rule describes.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How long before you can reapply after a monetization rejection?</div><div class="a">Thirty days after a first rejection, and ninety days after any subsequent one. If a suspension is scheduled, you can appeal within seven days before it takes effect, or within twenty-one days if it has already happened, and YouTube states it responds within fourteen days.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Do TikTok and Meta have the same AI rules as YouTube?</div><div class="a">No, and the differences matter. TikTok requires labelling of realistic synthetic scenes and bans synthetic media carrying the visual or audio likeness of a real person. Meta's 2026 originality update is medium-agnostic and does not mention AI. In the EU, Article 50 of the AI Act has applied since 2 August 2026.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cav">Where every quote came from</div>
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1311392" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube channel monetization policies</a>: the quality principles, Generic or Repetitive Content, Reused content, and AI Personas Related to Sensitive Topics, plus the July 2025 changelog entry describing the rename.</li>
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/14328491" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube disclosure of altered or synthetic content</a>: the photorealistic trigger, the exemption list including cloning your own voice, the non-disclosure penalty, and the statement that disclosure does not limit audience or earnings.</li>
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9235730" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube Partner Programme rejections</a> and <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9564590" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">appeals</a>: the 30 and 90 day reapplication windows, the 7 and 21 day appeal windows, and the 14 day response time.</li>
      <li><a href="https://blog.youtube/inside-youtube/the-future-of-youtube-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube CEO annual letter, 21 January 2026</a>: more than one million channels using AI creation tools daily, and the stated approach to low quality AI content.</li>
      <li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/09/youtube-prepares-crackdown-on-mass-produced-and-repetitive-videos-as-concern-over-ai-slop-grows" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TechCrunch, 9 July 2025</a> and <a href="https://www.socialmediatoday.com/news/youtube-clarifies-monetization-update-inauthentic-repeated-content/752892/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Social Media Today, 13 July 2025</a>: YouTube's Creator Liaison describing the update as minor and longstanding.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.kapwing.com/blog/ai-slop-report-the-global-rise-of-low-quality-ai-videos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The vendor study, November 2025</a>: hand-categorised sample of a new account's feed, with the channels named. Self-published by a company selling video tools, with its method stated.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.infobae.com/america/agencias/2026/01/29/varios-canales-de-ia-desaparecen-de-youtube-tras-el-anuncio-de-la-plataforma-de-reducir-contenido-de-baja-calidad/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Europa Press wire report, 29 January 2026</a>: at least sixteen channels removed or emptied, reported as observation rather than confirmation.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.ibtimes.co.uk/youtube-cracks-down-ai-generated-content-1800213" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBTimes UK, 2 June 2026</a>: the article that credits the examples to The Verge without a link, and sources its revenue figure to a content farm.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/community-guidelines/en/integrity-authenticity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TikTok integrity and authenticity guidelines</a>: labelling of realistic synthetic media and the ban on the likeness of a real person.</li>
      <li><a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/03/rewarding-original-creators-on-facebook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta on rewarding original creators, 13 March 2026</a>: the unoriginal content rules, written without reference to AI.</li>
      <li><a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 50</a>: the deployer duty for deep fake audio and video, the creative work exemption, and the editorial responsibility carve-out for text.</li>
